"A couplet is a pair of lines. The two lines have the same number of characters, and their words match each other one by one. One line goes on each side of the door. A short line, the horizontal scroll, goes above the door. Many families also hang a diamond of red paper with just one character, such as 春 for spring or 福 for good fortune.": "ea1ce4aba37b",
"A few days before the Lunar New Year, something red appears on doors all over Taiwan. Families put up strips of red paper with lucky words written in ink. These are spring couplets.": "1870cf82efb0",
"A few days before the Lunar New Year, something red appears on doors all over Taiwan. Families put up strips of red paper with lucky words written in ink. These are spring couplets. The custom is very old. Long ago, people hung two boards of peach wood by the door at the New Year to keep bad things away. Later they wrote lucky lines on the boards. The history of the Song dynasty tells of a king of Shu, Meng Chang, who wrote his own pair of lines on the boards one New Year's Eve in the tenth century. In time, red paper took the place of wood. A couplet is a pair of lines. The two lines have the same number of characters, and their words match each other one by one. One line goes on each side of the door. A short line, the horizontal scroll, goes above the door. Many families also hang a diamond of red paper with just one character, such as 春 for spring or 福 for good fortune. Which line goes on which side? Listen to the last character. The upper line ends in an oblique tone, and the lower line ends in a level tone. In Mandarin, the first and second tones are mostly level, and the third and fourth tones are oblique. By custom, the upper line goes on the right as you face the door. There is one trap. A few characters, such as 福, are second tone in Mandarin today, but they belong to an old group of short, clipped sounds that count as oblique. That is why some people check a tricky line by saying it in Taiwanese or Hakka. You can write your own. Fold the red paper lightly to mark a square for each character, write from top to bottom, and let the ink dry flat. Every winter, calligraphers at museums and halls around Taiwan write couplets for visitors. This year, try writing one yourself.": "0ae56f232b05",
